zebkit, Windows: timers that fire under an application's own message loop

The run loop of this port fires its timers from RunLoop::run(): a loop
that looks at the timers, takes the messages off the queue and waits
until the next timer is due. The port's own programs run it. An
application that embeds the engine has a message loop of its own
(GetMessage and DispatchMessage, or that of its toolkit), cannot run
this one, which is not in the API, and so never fired a timer: nothing in
the UI process that waits for a moment ever went on. The CDP endpoint
waits in many places, and so does WebKit.

When a timer is started the run loop's window is already sent a message,
which did nothing but wake the loop. It now fires what is due and sets a
timer of the system for the next, and does the same when that arrives.
Messages to a window reach it whoever dispatches them, so this works
under any loop, RunLoop::run() included.

diff --git a/Source/WTF/wtf/win/RunLoopWin.cpp b/Source/WTF/wtf/win/RunLoopWin.cpp
index ef92d7281..43422ff41 100644
--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/win/RunLoopWin.cpp
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/win/RunLoopWin.cpp
@@ -58,7 +58,19 @@ LRESULT RunLoop::wndProc(HWND hWnd, UINT message, WPARAM wParam, LPARAM lParam)
         performWork();
         return 0;
     case SetTimerMessage:
+    case WM_TIMER: {
+        // zebkit: the timers are fired by RunLoop::run(), and an application
+        // has a message loop of its own and does not run that one. A timer
+        // of the system brings the loop here when the next one is due,
+        // whoever takes the messages off the queue.
+        fireTimers();
+        DWORD delay = msTillNextTimer();
+        if (delay < 3600 * 1000)
+            ::SetTimer(hWnd, 1, delay, nullptr);
+        else
+            ::KillTimer(hWnd, 1);
         return 0;
+    }
     case FireTimerMessage:
         RunLoop::TimerBase* timer = nullptr;
         timer = std::bit_cast<RunLoop::TimerBase*>(wParam);
